# Build Your First Action

This tutorial builds an Action that totals an invoice, then adds a schema, an output contract, a failure case, runtime context, and an effect request. An Action is one named unit of work, and it runs without an Agent or a Jido instance.

## Install Jido

```elixir
Mix.install([
  {:jido, github: "agentjido/jido", branch: "release/v3"}
])
```

## Write the smallest Action

`use Jido.Action` needs a `:name`. `run/2` receives the input and a context map, and it returns `{:ok, map}`.

```elixir
defmodule FirstAction.TotalDraft do
  use Jido.Action, name: "first_action_total_draft"

  @impl true
  def run(%{lines: lines}, _context) do
    {:ok, %{subtotal_cents: Enum.sum(lines)}}
  end
end

Jido.Exec.run(FirstAction.TotalDraft, %{lines: [1_200, 300]})
```

The cell returns `{:ok, %{subtotal_cents: 1500}}`.

`Jido.Exec.run/4` is the normal way to run an Action. Calling `run/2` directly skips validation and error handling.

## Add a schema

This Action has no contract yet, so bad input reaches `run/2`. Add an input schema and an output schema with `Jido.Joi`. Exec checks both, and a default in the input schema fills in a missing value.

```elixir
defmodule FirstAction.TotalInvoice do
  alias Jido.Joi

  use Jido.Action,
    name: "first_action_total_invoice",
    description: "Adds tax to the invoice lines",
    schema:
      Joi.object(%{
        lines: Joi.list(Joi.integer() |> Joi.min(0)),
        tax_percent: Joi.integer() |> Joi.default(10)
      }),
    output_schema: Joi.object(%{total_cents: Joi.integer()})

  @impl true
  def run(%{lines: lines, tax_percent: tax_percent}, _context) do
    subtotal = Enum.sum(lines)
    {:ok, %{total_cents: subtotal + div(subtotal * tax_percent, 100)}}
  end
end

Jido.Exec.run(FirstAction.TotalInvoice, %{lines: [1_200, 300]})
```

The cell returns `{:ok, %{total_cents: 1650}}`. The default tax of 10 percent applied.

Invalid input stops before `run/2`. The error is a value with a path to the field.

```elixir
{:error, %Jido.Error.ValidationError{details: %{errors: [invalid]}}} =
  Jido.Exec.run(FirstAction.TotalInvoice, %{lines: "none"})

invalid.path
```

The cell returns `[:lines]`.

The same schemas describe the Action to other code. `authoring_contract/0` returns them in JSON Schema form.

```elixir
FirstAction.TotalInvoice.authoring_contract()["input_schema"]["properties"] |> Map.keys()
```

The cell returns `["lines", "tax_percent"]`.

## Return a failure

Return `{:error, reason}` when the work cannot finish. Exec wraps the reason in a `Jido.Error.ExecutionError`. Use `Jido.Error.execution_error/2` to add your own details and to mark the failure as not worth retrying.

```elixir
defmodule FirstAction.ChargeInvoice do
  alias Jido.Joi

  use Jido.Action,
    name: "first_action_charge_invoice",
    schema: Joi.object(%{total_cents: Joi.integer()})

  @impl true
  def run(%{total_cents: total}, %{credit_limit_cents: limit}) when total > limit do
    {:error, Jido.Error.execution_error("over credit limit", %{reason: :over_limit, retry: false})}
  end

  def run(%{total_cents: total}, _context), do: {:ok, %{charged_cents: total}}
end
```

## Pass context

The third argument to `Jido.Exec.run/4` is the context. Put runtime dependencies and caller data there, and keep input for the work itself. Context is not validated by the Action's schema.

```elixir
{:ok, %{charged_cents: 1650}} =
  Jido.Exec.run(FirstAction.ChargeInvoice, %{total_cents: 1_650}, %{credit_limit_cents: 5_000})

{:error, %Jido.Error.ExecutionError{message: message, details: %{reason: reason}}} =
  Jido.Exec.run(FirstAction.ChargeInvoice, %{total_cents: 9_000}, %{credit_limit_cents: 5_000})

{message, reason}
```

The cell returns `{"over credit limit", :over_limit}`. Match on the struct and on `details`, because messages can change.

## Request an effect

An Action can perform I/O itself. It can also return a third element that lists work for its caller. Exec carries the list and does not act on it.

```elixir
defmodule FirstAction.SendInvoice do
  use Jido.Action,
    name: "first_action_send_invoice",
    schema: Jido.Joi.object(%{invoice_id: Jido.Joi.string()})

  @impl true
  def run(%{invoice_id: invoice_id}, _context) do
    {:ok, %{status: "sent"}, [{:email_customer, invoice_id}]}
  end
end

Jido.Exec.run(FirstAction.SendInvoice, %{invoice_id: "inv-1001"})
```

The cell returns `{:ok, %{status: "sent"}, [email_customer: "inv-1001"]}`. Nothing has sent an email. Your code decides what to do with the request.

## Set a timeout and retries

The caller chooses execution policy, so the Action stays the same. This Action fails twice before it succeeds, and `max_attempts: 3` lets Exec run it three times. A failure with `retry: false`, like the credit limit above, does not retry.

```elixir
defmodule FirstAction.FetchExchangeRate do
  use Jido.Action, name: "first_action_fetch_exchange_rate"

  @impl true
  def run(_params, %{attempts: attempts}) do
    :counters.add(attempts, 1, 1)

    case :counters.get(attempts, 1) do
      attempt when attempt < 3 -> {:error, :rate_service_busy}
      attempt -> {:ok, %{usd_to_eur: 0.92, attempt: attempt}}
    end
  end
end

Jido.Exec.run(FirstAction.FetchExchangeRate, %{}, %{attempts: :counters.new(1, [])},
  max_attempts: 3,
  timeout: 1_000
)
```

The cell returns `{:ok, %{attempt: 3, usd_to_eur: 0.92}}`.

